From 27d05e9bb32f319c80c4aee66c0448358a928fda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lateautumn233 <lateautumn233@foxmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 07:11:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] DroidVM macOS: WindowServer as render server, 24 clients,
 raised object limits, 64-entry submissions, no-pool BOs

What scripts/build-droidvm-gpu.py of the macOS VM did to the staged sources as string edits, now the sources themselves.

- The render server is WindowServer (iOS's is backboardd): it takes slot 0's large window and is not capped. 24 connections instead
  of six (after login about twelve processes keep one; with twelve the later ones failed to open the GPU), each other window 1 GiB,
  24 GiB of the VBO's 64 GiB (at 256 MiB the icon agent's CoreImage textures ran out of addresses); 32 opens per process name per
  boot instead of 8 (the login UI is relaunched with each WindowServer).
- Object limits 4096 per client and 8192 GPU mappings instead of 1024 (WindowServer and the agents ran out of mappings after ten or
  twenty minutes logged in); the binding table on the heap (it no longer fit the collection's room for a native kext's __DATA); the
  mapping table's walks stop at the highest slot used and a handle's lookup tries its last slot first (11 us less a submission).
- Submissions of up to 64 command stream entries instead of 16 (ABI buffers, framing, a job's distinct buffers: at 16 a larger
  submission wrote into the next job and a close poisoned every client's GPU).
- A submission releases the jobs the ring has seen complete and asks the host only when every slot is taken (86 to 8 us before the
  ring in a window drag).
- dropViews releases the map removeMappingForDescriptor hands back: its user PTEs outlived clientClose, so releaseAll freed mapped
  pages and pmap_assert_free panicked an exiting GPU client without the pool.
- Without the pool (droidvm-gpu-pool-mib=0): a probe page learns whether host imports are IO-coherent; with write combining off the
  GPU's own BOs are mapped write-back coherent and the device reports cached coherent memory; the fallback no longer searches
  contiguous pages for a BO that need not be, backs a failing contiguous size off for 2 s, and a large BO's contiguous first try
  takes the pool only; user BOs outside the pool are VM pages with no kernel mapping (vm_page_alloc_list, maintained through the
  physical aperture): IOBufferMemoryDescriptor filled the kernel's 1.5 GiB DATA_SHARED range at a first login.
- Bring-up logging: a failed call names the process, method, status and window peak; window peaks each 64 MiB; pinned memory
  outside the pool beside the pool's use.
